WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
Validation is an explicit part of the
security boundary.

2
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:13.531
Minimal API handlers should validate
required fields, type assumptions,

3
00:00:13.611 --> 00:00:21.662
ranges, lengths, formats, and business
rules before performing state changes or

4
00:00:21.742 --> 00:00:24.775
calling downstream services.

5
00:00:24.855 --> 00:00:32.906
A handler that accepts bound input and
immediately changes state is relying on too

6
00:00:32.986 --> 00:00:35.086
much implicit trust.

7
00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:39.133
Validation should match the operation.

8
00:00:39.213 --> 00:00:45.747
A search endpoint may validate query
length, allowed sort fields,

9
00:00:45.827 --> 00:00:48.161
and pagination limits.

10
00:00:48.241 --> 00:00:55.591
A create endpoint may validate required
fields and business constraints.

11
00:00:55.671 --> 00:01:02.322
An update endpoint may validate which
fields are allowed to change.

12
00:01:02.402 --> 00:01:10.336
A tenant-scoped endpoint may validate that
the resource belongs to the expected

13
00:01:10.416 --> 00:01:12.633
tenant before acting.

14
00:01:12.713 --> 00:01:20.763
Endpoint filters can support reusable
cross-cutting checks such as validation,

15
00:01:20.843 --> 00:01:26.677
logging context, policy preconditions,
request shaping,

16
00:01:26.757 --> 00:01:30.257
and consistent failure responses.

17
00:01:30.337 --> 00:01:36.288
Filters are powerful because they can
reduce repeated code,

18
00:01:36.368 --> 00:01:42.085
but they should be clear, narrow, and easy
to reason about.

19
00:01:42.165 --> 00:01:47.999
A filter that does too much can become a
hidden policy layer.

20
00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:56.130
Validation failures should return
consistent client-facing responses without

21
00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:00.060
exposing sensitive internal details.

22
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.340
Clients need to know what they can
correct.

23
00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:10.837
They do not need stack traces, internal
type names, secret names,

24
00:02:10.917 --> 00:02:15.585
connection details, or implementation
paths.

25
00:02:15.665 --> 00:02:23.832
Protected logs can preserve operational
context that should not be returned to the

26
00:02:23.912 --> 00:02:24.729
caller.

27
00:02:24.809 --> 00:02:30.642
Validation metadata and OpenAPI
descriptions are useful,

28
00:02:30.722 --> 00:02:38.190
but they do not prove runtime enforcement
unless validation actually runs.

29
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:46.437
A schema that marks a field as required
helps documentation and client generation.

30
00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:52.818
The server still needs to enforce the rule
when requests arrive.

31
00:02:52.898 --> 00:03:00.482
The practical baseline is to make
validation visible in the endpoint design.

32
00:03:00.562 --> 00:03:08.612
Use DTOs, validators, endpoint filters, or
service-layer checks consistently.

33
00:03:08.692 --> 00:03:15.460
Make state-changing operations earn trust
before they modify data,

34
00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:21.140
call external services, or return
sensitive responses.
